    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. There's only one test class (`AcceptanceTest`), plus helper base classes, and it passed along with a full compile.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag so it's `true` when an owner's city already holds **40\u201349** owners at creation time (approaching the hard limit of 50), while the existing hard rejection at 50 (HTTP 409) is unchanged.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added a `CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40` constant. Captured the existing per-city count (`cityOwnerCount`) into a variable reused by both the hard check and the new warning, and set `owner.setCapacityWarning(cityOwnerCount >= 40)` alongside where the other warning flags are set at create time. (At that point the count is guaranteed < 50, since \u226550 already returned 409.)\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the persisted `capacityWarning` `Boolean` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter/setter, mirroring `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the owner schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it (MapStruct auto-maps the same-named field, matching how `bulkSignupWarning` works).\n\n4. **Schema files** (`db/h2`, `db/hsqldb`, `db/mysql`, `db/postgres`) \u2014 Added the `capacity_warning` column to the `owners` table so the flag survives the create\u2192read round trip the test exercises.\n\nThe flag is stored on the owner at creation, so the test's `GET /api/owners/{id}` reads back `capacityWarning: true` after filling a fresh city to 40 and creating the 41st owner.",
